Terraform Tip: What Does terraform import Do — and How Do You Use It?
Ever created AWS resources manually (via the AWS Console) and later thought:
“I wish I could manage this with Terraform — without deleting and starting over.”
That’s where terraform import shines. 🌟
🧠 Imagine This:
You move into a fully furnished apartment — everything's already set up.
Now, you want to create a digital list of everything inside so you can manage it better.
🛋️ Sofa → Add to inventory
 📺 TV → Add to inventory
That’s exactly what terraform import does:
“Terraform, that resource already exists. Just take control of it.”
✅ Why Use terraform import?
✔️ Bring manually created AWS resources under Terraform
 ✔️ Avoid deleting and re-creating working services
 ✔️ Begin full Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) management
 ✔️ Clean up “mixed environments” of manual + automated setups
🔧 How to Use It (Simple Steps):
1️⃣ Write a matching Terraform block
 (e.g. for an existing S3 bucket):
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
  bucket = "my-existing-bucket"
}
2️⃣ Run the import command:
terraform import aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket my-existing-bucket
3️⃣ Run terraform plan to confirm Terraform now tracks it.
That’s it! 🎯
🧩 Bonus Tip:
You can import:
EC2 instances
S3 buckets
VPCs
Security groups
IAM roles
 …and many more AWS resources!
🗣️ In Simple Words:
"terraform import is like telling Terraform:
 ‘That thing already exists — now you take care of it going forward.’"
Make your cloud setup smarter — no need to start from scratch!
